last speaker is christina you christina  is an assistant professor at  cornell and a research fellow in the  reinforcement learning program  thanks prasad um so in this  talk we're going to look at the problem  of episodic reinforcement learning with  continuous state in action spaces  in which we have an agent interacting  with an environment and  the dynamics and the reward of function  associated to the environment are  unknown so the agent is trying to  learn the optimal policy as it interacts  with the environment but in this  setting we want to deal with the case of  a continuous state in action space  where you have to figure out how to  reduce the complexity of your space in  order to learn  so one approach is to use parametric  function approximators but these  suffer model mismatch loss and require  prior knowledge of the structure in  order to  guarantee that that  guarantee your convergence with respect  to a particular model class  so instead we're going to look at  non-parametric  assumptions on the value function with  respect to some known metric  and next slide  so one naive approach to deal with the  continuous space is to  simply uniformly discretize the space to  map it to a tabular  mdp and often time this this uh  discretization  parameter epsilon is chosen as a  function of the time horizon to balance  the approximation error and the cost of  solving the tableau ndp  this discretization can be very  expensive though  next slide so  we propose an adaptive discrimination  algorithm which  essentially starts with a course  discussion of the space  and only refines the partition as needed  in a data dependent way  so this generates kind of a sequence of  tabular ndps that  that locally refines the the discussion  only in areas of high value  so this reduces memory requirements and  accelerates the learning  um and i'll illustrate some properties  of the algorithm just through  simulations  next slide so in this problem  um we're consider as a simple oil  discovery problem where there's an agent  surveying a map in search of hidden oil  deposits and it collects reward  um according to a um  the cost of transportation cost cost of  traveling on this  map as well as a surveying reward  um related to how close you are to this  uh kind of hidden oil  um and to make it easy to visualize  we've chosen um  the unit interval to be the state in  action space um  on the bottom left these two plots um  are illustrating the the q function  which is the  expected value of the optimal policy for  being in a state in action  pair and green denotes areas of higher  value and red denotes area of low value  and overlaid on top of these plots  is the partition that our algorithm  discovers so  the left is for the model free version  of algorithm the right is for the model  based version of our algorithm  and you can see that the partition our  algorithm generates does in fact  focus in on the regions of high value  and on the right side  on the right top plot you can see that  when you that when you compare the  the reward a collective reward from our  algorithm as opposed to a naive uniform  discretization algorithm um our  algorithm in fact performs much better  converging faster to the optimal policy  and on the bottom right it's showing  that our algorithm impacts has  a significant gains in memory savings so  it has a significantly smaller size of  partition and next slide  so in this plot i'm just showing that uh  we chose a different function for the  reward function that's  significantly more peaked um meaning  that the  as you can see in the q function um that  there is a  narrow region with high rewards so it's  kind of looking for a needle in a  haystack  but our algorithm is indeed able to kind  of locate this narrow region of high  reward as opposed to the uniform epsilon  that  um does not converge and in fact does  not achieve the optimum policy as you  can see  from the reward plot on the upper right  um okay so that's  uh that's that's it there's one more  example in the next slide but i'll um  just show this slide and not talk about  it welcome to  ask me about it in the next breakout  sessions
 

